+Traps this encodes, each paid for on a real boot:
+
+  * moe-cache pool (2026-08-31): take the LAST report per (device, pool index).
+    A drafter load triggers a SECOND allocation and both are logged; summing all
+    matches double-counts (measured 13,947 against a live 6,820 -- 2.05x).
+  * weights / KV / state: SUM every report per device. A GPU-pinned drafter logs
+    the SAME line shapes as the target model and both allocations are LIVE at
+    once, so last-wins let the drafter overwrite the target -- `model=` reported
+    656 MiB on a card whose target model held 3,313, while the drafter's
+    recurrent state matched nothing at all. Both vanished into `unaccounted`
+    (6,082 of 22,992 MiB -- 84% of that gap is under-attribution, not genuine
+    overhead, and it read as a CUDA1-vs-CUDA0 routing asymmetry). `kv` was
+    already summed; `model` and `state` now are too.
   * A pool figure read at boot is a LOWER BOUND. The pool grows into free VRAM
     as traffic admits experts, until it hits the free-minus-reserve floor.
 
+Known residual: the drafter's COMPUTE reservation. `compute` keeps last-wins,
+which is the right rule for a single model -- the target RE-reserves after the
+drafter loads and that is the same buffer again, so summing would double-count
+the re-reserve. The log does not label which context a `sched_reserve` line
+belongs to, so a drafter's reservation cannot be separated from a re-reserve by
+position alone (target 5208.35 -> drafter 1606.48 -> target re-reserve 5240.16
+on the same card); the drafter's share stays in `unaccounted` until the engine
+labels the reservation.
+
 Scope: the BYTE split only. Pool slot counts, hit rates and cache health belong to
 CAPTURE: EXPERT CACHE and are deliberately not duplicated here -- one owner per
 number, so the two sections can never disagree.
